# Commit Message Rules  
- Use the company commit format: `<type>(<scope>): <short description>`  
- Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `chore`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`,   
`test`, `build`, `ci`, `revert`, `hotfix`  
- Include scope when relevant: `api`, `ui`, `auth`, `database`, `config`,   
`docs`, `tests`  
- Keep subject line concise, under 50 characters  
- Use imperative mood: "Add feature" not "Added feature"  
- Avoid vague messages; be clear and descriptive  
- Reference issues/tickets using `#` prefix when applicable (e.g., `#123`)  
- Use commit body for bullet points of key changes, explaining the "why" behind   
each change  
- Mark breaking changes explicitly with `BREAKING CHANGE` footer if applicable  
# Examples for Each Commit Type  
- **feat (Feature)**   
 Example: `feat(auth): add user profile picture upload feature`   
 Why: Helps track new capabilities over time   
- **fix (Bug Fix)**   
 Example: `fix(ui): resolve crash issue on back navigation`   
 Why: Marks bug fixes, critical for QA and post-release review   
- **chore (Maintenance Task)**   
 Example: `chore: update project dependencies`   
 Why: Keeps project running smoothly without affecting features   
- **docs (Documentation)**   
 Example: `docs(api): add usage instructions for endpoints`   
 Why: Ensures documentation is up-to-date for onboarding and reference   
- **style (Code Style)**   
 Example: `style(ui): apply consistent formatting using linter`   
 Why: Improves code readability and reduces merge conflicts   
- **refactor (Code Refactoring)**   
 Example: `refactor(db): extract query logic to helper module`   
 Why: Makes code more maintainable   
- **perf (Performance Improvement)**   
 Example: `perf(image): lazy load images to reduce memory usage`   
 Why: Indicates focus on performance improvements   
- **test (Tests)**   
 Example: `test(auth): add integration tests for login flow`   
 Why: Ensures system robustness with new features   
- **build (Build System)**   
 Example: `build(android): update Gradle and CocoaPods configuration`   
 Why: Tracks changes to build or dependency setup   
- **ci (Continuous Integration)**   
 Example: `ci: update GitHub Actions to run tests on PRs`   
 Why: Helps maintain and improve automation processes   
- **revert (Reverting Commits)** 
 Example: `revert: revert 'feat(auth): add OAuth login'`   
 Why: Quickly identifies rolled-back changes   
- **hotfix (Critical Fix)**   
 Example: `hotfix(api): fix payment service outage`   
 Why: Marks critical or urgent fixes for prioritization   
# Additional Guidelines  
- Always include detailed bullet points in commit body if necessary   
- Explain the "why" behind each change   
- Reference tickets/issues   
- Mark breaking changes clearly with a footer 